  "query_preview": "(This evening through Friday)...\nIssued at 249 PM EDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nToday through Sunday... \n\nA synoptic boundary will continue to work its way into central \nIndiana tonight into tomorrow, with a large theta-e gradient across \nthe state tomorrow. This gradient will generally be aligned just \nnorth of the I-74 corridor with temperatures in the low 80s and \nupper 70s to the N/E and upper 80s to the S/W. \n\nThe convection overnight created a mesoscale boundary, and has \nstalled over SW portions of the state. This is being reinforced by \ndifferential heating this afternoon, and will be the primary focus \nfor additional thunderstorm development over the next 6-8 hours. The \naforementioned synoptic boundary will slowly push to the SW with \ntime, of which will eventually will impede and push convection to \nthe SW as well. Until then, a broad 20-30% PoPs will be in the \nforecast for areas SW of the I-74 corridor. \n\nThis all said, there is a more organized area of convection over \neastern Illinois as of 1830Z. This convection has been able to for a \ncold pool, and should be able to sustain itself as it enters central \nIndiana between 3-4PM. For this reason 40-60% PoPs have been added \nfor western central Indiana. A fairly deep stable layer in the low \nlevels will likely try and push this cold pool southward with time. \nThere still some uncertainty if this convective system will be able \nto stay in balance as it continue to push through central Indiana as \nthe effective shear is rather limited with eastern extent. The lack \nof shear and strong surface lapse rates should keep the severe wind \ngust threat small, but given the strong cold pool, isolated gusts to \n45MPH will be possible for a brief period this afternoon. \n\nThe remainder of the weekend will be a constant push/pull of this \nlingering stationary boundary over the region creating both a minor \nswing in temperatures and persistent rain chances. The best \nchances for rain will be Saturday afternoon as a weak shortwave \nmoves SE over the stationary boundary. This wave will increase \noverall shear across the area enough to support a few organized \nthunderstorm clusters and a marginal risk for damaging wind gusts. \n\nTemperatures are expected to remain warm, with some uncertainty \ndepending on boundary placement and cloud cover extent; general \nexpectations are for highs in the upper 80s each afternoon for \nIndianapolis.\n\nTuesday and Beyond... \n\nSurface high pressure will nudge into the area mid to late week next \nweek with mostly dry weather likely Wednesday onward. Temperature \nare also expected to drop much closer to seasonal for the middle of \nnext week with highs in the mid 70s and lows in the 50s. Will have \nto watch for an inverted trough just to the east, which could bring \na few showers if it ends up being farther west than expected.",
